description:
Background: Several customers and internal e2e tests surfaced flaky streaming output from the OpenAI-compat endpoint. Symptoms included: (1) clients receiving invalid/partial JSON objects because the SSE framing split JSON objects across events without a deterministic delimiter; (2) inconsistent ordering where end-of-stream markers or final tokens arrived before buffered structural tokens, causing parsers to fail; (3) double-CRLF and missing-data delimiters in certain backpressure/flush paths. Motivation: Streaming stability is critical for downstream SDKs (JS/Python) and for structured output / tool-calling. This PR fixes the root framing issues in the SSE pipeline, hardens the OpenAI-compat adapter, and adds unit/integration tests to prevent regressions. Summary of changes: - Implement deterministic event framing in sse encoder: always emit event lines prefixed with "data: " and ensure JSON chunks are emitted as complete units when possible; partial tokens are buffered and re-emitted with an explicit continuation marker. - Normalize newline delimiters: replace mixed CRLF/LF behavior with a single canonical "

" event separator and ensure end-of-stream emits the standard "data: [DONE]" frame used by OpenAI compatibility. - Ensure token ordering guarantees: rework stream flush logic so that structural tokens (braces/brackets/delimiters) are not dropped ahead of trailing tokens; introduce a small reorder-safe buffer and flush policy. - Fix adapter edge-cases in openai_compat/stream_adapter: escape embedded delimiter sequences in structured outputs, and avoid injecting extra "data: " prefixes when wrapping multi-line events. - Tests: add unit tests covering partial JSON across events, delimiter normalization, ordering under backpressure, and an integration test exercising the JS SDK against the local compat endpoint. - Docs: document framing rules and SDK expectations in docs/streaming.md. Checklist: - [x] Reproduce flaky behavior with a deterministic e2e test harness - [x] Implement encoder/adapter fixes - [x] Add unit + integration tests - [x] Update docs and add changelog entry - [x] Fix linter/unit failures from first CI run Files changed (high level): - redwood/serving/sse/encoder.py: deterministic framing, buffering API, new helper functions. - redwood/serving/sse/decoder.py: tolerate continuation markers on ingress. - redwood/api/openai_compat/stream_adapter.py: escape/normalize framing for compat layer. - redwood/api/handlers/stream_handler.py: adjusted flush hooks and backpressure signaling. - redwood/tests/unit/test_sse_framing.py: unit tests for partial JSON/token boundaries. - redwood/tests/integration/test_streaming_openai_compat.py: end-to-end compat test. - redwood/docs/streaming.md: doc updates for framing contract. - redwood/console/observability/metrics_streaming.md: add a note about stream error metrics. - redwood-ci/workflows/streaming.yml: add a streaming e2e job. Commits (summary): - 8f3c2a1: reproduce: add e2e harness that simulates partial-frame splits - 3b9a6e4: sse: deterministic framing and buffering primitives - d1a4f7b: compat: normalize/escape framing in openai adapter - 6ac4f12: tests: add unit + integration tests for framing and ordering - 2f7d9b0: docs: streaming.md + changelog - 4c0f5d3: ci: add streaming e2e job and fix flakey runner Notes on implementation: - The encoder now exposes a small API: push_token(token: bytes, is_structural: bool) -> Optional[event_chunk] to allow callers to indicate structural tokens that must be coalesced. - For partial JSON, we now buffer until a structural boundary (comma, brace, bracket) or a size/time threshold is reached; in the latter case we emit a continuation event with an explicit continuation marker in the data payload so clients can reassemble safely. - The openai_compat adapter will never insert extra "data: " prefixes into an already-framed payload; instead it wraps the payload into a single framed event or emits multiple properly delimited events. - Backpressure paths in the stream handler now prefer draining structural tokens first to avoid truncated JSON objects in the client. Backwards compatibility: the outer SSE framing follows OpenAI convention (data: <json>

) and the final frame is data: [DONE]. Clients using our SDKs should be unaffected; non-compliant custom clients that relied on previous quirks may need minor fixes. Testing: added unit and integration tests; performed local JS SDK run and CI streaming workflow. CI: the first run failed linting around trailing commas; addressed and re-ran. All checks are passing on merge.
